{"schema":"plectis_maths_universe_excerpts_v2","paper":"erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface","excerpts":[["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-i11a","Small remainder forces the greedy\nword","<p><em> Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> and let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></math></span> be nonnegative integer\nweights satisfying </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Fix an integer capacity\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>C</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>. The greedy word <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>γ</mi></math></span> is obtained by taking <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math></span> exactly when it does not exceed the\nremaining capacity. For an admissible Boolean word <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>ε</mi></math></span>, meaning <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>C</mi></math></span>, one has\n</em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟺</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mi>ε</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>γ</mi><mtext> </mtext><mtext>and</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Indeed, two different words have sums\nseparated by at least <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>g</mi></math></span>, as seen at\ntheir first differing index; the greedy sum is the largest admissible\nsum. Thus there is at most one admissible word with remainder below\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>g</mi></math></span>, and any such word is greedy. The\ngreedy word alone need not have remainder below <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>g</mi></math></span>: for weights <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>, gap <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> and capacity <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></math></span>, its remainder is <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>3</mn></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-i11b","Gap domination of the integer\nweights","<p><em> For integers <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>M</mi></math></span>, the quotient weights satisfy </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mfrac><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>M</mi></msup><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow></munderover><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mfrac><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>M</mi></msup><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>j</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em>\nThus the finite weight list at ranks <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi></math></span> satisfies the hypothesis of\nTheorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#record:257bm-i11a\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-i11a\">6.100</a>\nwith <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></msup></math></span>. At <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>R</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> this gap is <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></span>, and at <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>R</mi></math></span> it is <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>R</mi></msup></math></span>. The inequality is unconditional; it\ndoes not assert that the greedy remainder is smaller than the\ngap.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-i11c","Uniqueness of the finite greedy\nrepresentation","<p><em> Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>M</mi></math></span>,\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>M</mi></math></span>, and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></msup></math></span>. If </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mfrac><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>M</mi></msup><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>,</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em> then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>ε</mi></math></span> is the greedy word for\ncapacity <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi></math></span> is its remainder. This follows by\napplying Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#record:257bm-i11a\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-i11a\">6.100</a> with the\npreceding gap inequality. The statement identifies any such\nrepresentation; it does not prove that a representation with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></msup></math></span> exists. The degenerate\nformal case <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span> has empty word and\nzero target, using truncated natural-number subtraction.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-i12","A division-free form of the binary\nbound","<p><em> For <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>M</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>, </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mfrac><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>M</mi></msup><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">⌊</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">⌋</mo></mrow></munderover><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em> where an empty sum\nis zero. To see this, write <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></math></span>\nwith <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi></math></span> and expand the\nfinite geometric sum. The remaining fraction is <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>r</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>, which lies strictly between\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>0</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></span>. Substitution in Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#record:257bm-i5\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-i5\">6.93</a> expresses its\nquotient condition as a finite sum of powers of <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>2</mn></math></span>. This is an exact rewriting, not a\nweaker hypothesis or a new existence result.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-i2","An upper bound for a binary coefficient\ntail","<p><em> Let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi><mo>:</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span> satisfy <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></math></span> for every <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi></math></span>. Then </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Indeed, <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></math></span>, while <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mi>r</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>. Thus the scaled\ntail is <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>, and in particular\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>, as required in\nTheorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#record:257bm-i-t7\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-i-t7\">6.105</a>.\nThe coefficient bound also holds for Euler’s totient function.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-i-t7","Rationality through an integer\nrecurrence","<p><em> Let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi><mo>:</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span> satisfy <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></math></span> for every <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi></math></span>. Then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></math></span> is rational if and\nonly if there exist a positive integer <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>v</mi></math></span> and a sequence <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>u</mi><mo>:</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math></span> such that </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mfrac><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>N</mi></msup></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟶</mo><mn>0.</mn></math></span></div>\n<p><em> The limit condition is <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>; it does not assume that\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>u</mi></math></span> is bounded. No divisor-count\nhypothesis is imposed on <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi></math></span>. Thus\nthe ordinary statement also applies to <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>φ</mi></math></span>; the identification with the\nseparate formal #249 development, including its indexing conventions, is\nnot asserted here.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-i-mob","Möbius inversion of the divisor\ncounts","<p><em> For a set <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>A</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span> and each positive\ninteger <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi></math></span>, </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em> where <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>μ</mi></math></span> is the Möbius function and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> counts the positive elements of\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>A</mi></math></span> dividing <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi></math></span>. Thus the divisor counts determine the\npositive support exactly. In particular their Möbius transform takes\nonly the values <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>0</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></span>. The identity does not depend on a\nbase.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-i-bridge","The support series as a coefficient\nseries","<p><em> For <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>A</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></math></span>, </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></munder><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mfrac><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Expanding each\ndenominator as a geometric series and interchanging nonnegative sums\ngives the identity. Since <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></math></span>, Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#record:257bm-i-t7\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-i-t7\">6.105</a> applies. Together\nwith Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#record:257bm-i-mob\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-i-mob\">6.106</a>, it expresses\nrationality through an integer recurrence whose coefficients all come\nfrom the same support.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257rig-i2","A restriction on dyadic rational\nvalues","<p><em> If an infinite support <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>A</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></math></span> has <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi></msup></math></span> for integers <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>, then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></munder><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>a</mi></math></span> either diverges or\nconverges to a value greater than <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></span>. The cited proof averages the shifted\ninteger recurrence and uses a common multiple of two distinct support\nelements. Thus a convergent reciprocal sum of at most <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></span> is excluded. This is a separate\nnecessary condition: the reciprocal-summable criterion proved earlier\nalready excludes every support with a convergent reciprocal sum, not\njust those whose sum is at most <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257rig-i3","Unboundedness of a positive shifted\nrecurrence","<p><em> Let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>A</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></math></span> be infinite,\nfix a shift <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span> and a positive\ninteger <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>v</mi></math></span>, and let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>u</mi></math></span> be a positive integer sequence\nsatisfying </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>v</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>u</mi></math></span> is unbounded. Indeed, under a proposed\nbound <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>B</mi></math></span>, choose <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> distinct support elements and a\ncommon multiple larger than <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi></math></span>. At\nthe corresponding index the divisor count is at least <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, contradicting the recurrence. This\ndoes not conflict with Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#record:257bm-i-t7\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-i-t7\">6.105</a>, whose condition\nis <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>, not boundedness.\nAn unbounded sequence can still satisfy that limit.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257rig-i4a","Bounds for intervals with zero divisor\ncounts","<p><em> Suppose that <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi></msup><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> for an infinite positive\nsupport <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>A</mi></math></span> and an odd positive\ninteger <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>v</mi></math></span>. For every <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span> there is <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>B</mi></math></span> such that, for <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, a run of <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>h</mi></math></span> zero divisor counts starting after\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></math></span> satisfies <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>h</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>ε</mi><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi></math></span>. In fact this\nconclusion holds without rationality: fix any <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></math></span>. Every <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi></math></span> consecutive positive integers include a\nmultiple of <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi></math></span>, where <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></math></span> is positive. Hence <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>h</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, so one can take <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>. The zero-run conclusion therefore\nimposes no additional restriction on a fixed nonempty support,\nregardless of the tail estimates used in the linked proof.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257rig-i4b","A subpower bound for divisor\ncounts","<p><em> For positive integers <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>≤</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>⋅</mo><mi>n</mi></math></span>, or\nequivalently <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></msup><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></math></span>. The constant absorbs the finitely many primes\nbelow <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup></math></span>; for larger primes, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>ν</mi></msup></math></span> controls each factor\nof the divisor product. This estimate is independent of the support\nproblem. The elementary zero-run bound above already follows from a\nsingle positive support element, without this estimate or a\nrecurrence.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257rig-i5","Two distinct prime-power\ndifferences commute","<p><em> Lemma <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#lem:mixed-prime-power-layer\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#lem:mixed-prime-power-layer\">6.57</a> gives\nthe four-term expansion and its divisor-count interpretation.\nCommutation is elementary for arbitrary positive multipliers. The\nextraction formula uses distinct primes, <em>positive</em> exponents\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo movablelimits=\"true\">gcd</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>. The example <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>12</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span> shows the extracted coefficient\nexplicitly. No rationality statement about multiplicative subsequences\nfollows from commutation alone.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-i-cross2","Existence of a first crossing","<p><em>\nLet <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>E</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span> be\nfinite and suppose <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>.\nThere is a least <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></math></span> such that\n</em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>∩</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>∩</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mi>c</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4.</mn></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Positivity of the\nsummands makes the partial sums increasing. Their odd reduced\ndenominators exclude equality with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>, so the first crossing is strict on\nboth sides. Finally <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>21</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>, which excludes a\ncrossing before rank <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>4</mn></math></span>. This is the\nfinite crossing data used in the later exact-row constructions; the\nlower bound on <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi></math></span> and strictness are\nspecific to these weights.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257hg-i6","Nonnegative centred carries below the\nhalf-value","<p><em> If <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>∉</mo><mi>A</mi></math></span> and\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>, then </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>ihc</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em>\nIndeed, Lemma <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#lem:collapse-mech\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#lem:collapse-mech\">2.4</a> gives </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>ihc</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.</mn></math></span></div>\n<p><em> The first term is\nstrictly positive and the tail is nonnegative. Since the half-carry is\nan integer, it is at least <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></span>;\nsubtracting <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></span> proves the centred\nbound. Nonnegativity alone, without this strictness and integrality\nstep, would only give <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≥</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>.\nThe argument supplies the lower bound used with the conditional upper\nbound in Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#record:257rig-c16\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257rig-c16\">6.84</a>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257hg-i7","Three possibilities at a skipped\nendpoint","<p><em> For a rank <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>s</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></math></span>\nomitted by the real greedy support, let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></math></span> be as in Lemma <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#lem:skipped-endpoint-trichotomy\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#lem:skipped-endpoint-trichotomy\">6.45</a>.\nThat lemma identifies the actual prefix as <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></math></span> when <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span> and as <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></math></span> when <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>, with the exact remainder or\novershoot in each case. To obtain the nonnegative-margin condition in\nDefinition <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#record:257bm-c13\">6.81</a>,\nthe negative case would still have to be excluded at every required\nskipped rank. The trichotomy itself does not exclude it. In particular,\nthe value <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></math></span> excluded for <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></math></span> under the all-right-tail hypothesis\nof Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#thm:final-middle-cell\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:final-middle-cell\">6.44</a> is not an\nexclusion for <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></math></span>: the coordinates\nand the hypotheses are different.</em></p>"]],"source":"erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.json?v=054a6001eb83"}
